          "text": "If two tangents drawn from a point $(\\alpha,\\beta)$ lying on the ellipse $25x^2+4y^2=1$ to the parabola $y^2=4x$ are such that the slope of one tangent is four times the other, then the value of $(10\\alpha+5)^2+\\left(16\\beta^2+50\\right)^2$ equals ______.",
          "solution": "Tangent to $y^2=4x$: $y=mx+\\frac1m$. Through $(\\alpha,\\beta)$: $\\alpha m^2-\\beta m+1=0$. Roots $m$ and $4m$: sum $5m=\\frac\\beta\\alpha$, product $4m^2=\\frac1\\alpha$. Eliminating $m$: $4\\cdot\\frac{\\beta^2}{25\\alpha^2}=\\frac1\\alpha\\Rightarrow4\\beta^2=25\\alpha$. The point lies on the ellipse: $25\\alpha^2+4\\beta^2=1\\Rightarrow25\\alpha^2+25\\alpha=1\\Rightarrow\\alpha^2+\\alpha=\\frac1{25}$. Then $(10\\alpha+5)^2=100(\\alpha^2+\\alpha)+25=29$ and $16\\beta^2+50=100\\alpha+50=50(2\\alpha+1)$, so $\\left(16\\beta^2+50\\right)^2=2500(4\\alpha^2+4\\alpha+1)=2500\\cdot\\frac{29}{25}=2900$. Sum $=29+2900=2929$.<br><b>Answer: 2929</b>"
